    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="6" build="18345">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.6b18345</English>
                <French>v15.6b18345</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16" build="18345">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16b18345</English>
                <French>v1.16b18345</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Leakage Areas:



Along the areas below the baseboards. The hot water pipes are probably not sealed up well as it is an older home. There could also be some air getting in along the edges of the floor joist in the basement. May want to check and see if the vapor barrier has let go anywhere.



Sliding windows are quite leaky as the seal on most of them has worn out over time.



Sliding door is very leaky.



Attic hatch needs to be properly sealed with a gasket around the edges of the hatch. Add some weight (rigid insulatio) to the top of the hatch to improve insulation value of hatch and to help with compression.



Water and drain pipes under sinks are showing air leakage as they are not sealed properly.



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>If you plan on doing any major renovations, you should consider adding R-15 of Type 2 EPS rigid insulation to the exterior of the home.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on insulation upgrades and the criteria for these products.</mainWalls>
                <windows>Upgrade windows to ENERGY STAR® Zone 3 rated.



1. Replace large picture window in living room

2. Replace sliding windows in Main living area

3. Replace sliding windows in office area



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on windows/doors and the criteria for these products.





</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If there are air sealing upgrades completed to the home, it will make it tighter, but it will not allow for the home to breathe as it was before. A mechanical ventilation system such as an HRV/ERV should be installed to allow the home to be properly ventilated. Not installing a system after air sealing can lead to issues with mold and mildew.



The HRV/ERV will recover heated exhaust air via a heat exchanger and preheat supply air with the extracted heat, making the units very efficient. Make sure the unit is ENERGY STAR® rated and HVI certified.



HRV = Heat Recovery Ventilator

ERV = Enthalpy Recovery Ventilator



ERV also can control indoor humidity levels as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on ventilation systems and the criteria for these products.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Install a digital or smart thermostat to help reduce oil consumption.</HeatingSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
